WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.413
Option A puts vNIOS
members inside each cloud,

2
00:00:03.413 --> 00:00:06.688
as Grid members and
high-availability pairs.

3
00:00:06.688 --> 00:00:11.274
Infoblox's AWS reference design
puts the pair in a shared-services

4
00:00:11.274 --> 00:00:14.776
hub VPC that syncs with the
on-premises Grid Master.

5
00:00:14.776 --> 00:00:17.949
Signed zones stay signed:
the members serve them,

6
00:00:17.949 --> 00:00:20.250
but the keys stay on the Grid Master.

7
00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:22.888
How do cloud workloads reach vNIOS?

8
00:00:22.888 --> 00:00:24.612
Two documented patterns.

9
00:00:24.612 --> 00:00:28.876
Pattern one: point the
network's DNS at vNIOS.

10
00:00:28.876 --> 00:00:36.050
In AWS that's a DHCP option set; in Azure,
the virtual network's custom DNS setting.

11
00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:37.713
Simple, with a catch.

12
00:00:37.713 --> 00:00:40.644
In Azure, queries then
go straight to vNIOS,

13
00:00:40.644 --> 00:00:43.912
and Azure private zones
aren't consulted first.

14
00:00:43.912 --> 00:00:51.447
So vNIOS has to forward Azure-private
names back to Azure DNS at 168.63.129.16,

15
00:00:51.447 --> 00:00:55.700
or to a Private Resolver inbound endpoint.

16
00:00:55.700 --> 00:01:01.262
Pattern two: keep the cloud's own resolver
and forward chosen domains to vNIOS.

17
00:01:01.262 --> 00:01:05.870
In AWS, that's an outbound endpoint
and one forwarding rule per domain,

18
00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:08.538
and rules can be shared across accounts.

19
00:01:08.538 --> 00:01:12.212
In Azure, it's an outbound
endpoint and a forwarding ruleset,

20
00:01:12.212 --> 00:01:13.937
and clients change nothing.

21
00:01:13.937 --> 00:01:17.525
In Google, it's a forwarding
zone, which Google prefers.

22
00:01:17.525 --> 00:01:19.450
One AWS caution.

23
00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:24.192
If vNIOS has to resolve Route 53
private zones for other networks,

24
00:01:24.192 --> 00:01:26.475
forward to an inbound endpoint.

25
00:01:26.475 --> 00:01:29.924
AWS says forwarding to a
VPC's built-in plus-two

26
00:01:29.924 --> 00:01:34.100
resolver address from outside
that VPC isn't supported.

27
00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:39.373
And every vNIOS member is agency-operated
and inside the agency's boundary;

28
00:01:39.373 --> 00:01:41.925
no FedRAMP authorization covers it.

29
00:01:41.925 --> 00:01:47.966
Sources: Infoblox About vNIOS for
AWS, Shared-Services VPC Deployments,

30
00:01:47.966 --> 00:01:53.802
About vNIOS for Azure, Configure
vNIOS as Primary DNS for Azure VNets,

31
00:01:53.802 --> 00:01:59.945
vNIOS for Google Cloud; AWS DHCP option
sets, What is Route 53 VPC Resolver,

32
00:01:59.945 --> 00:02:06.703
Forwarding outbound queries, Resolving
queries between VPCs and your network;

33
00:02:06.703 --> 00:02:12.129
Microsoft Learn DNS Private
Resolver and VNet name resolution;

34
00:02:12.129 --> 00:02:17.930
Google Cloud DNS best practices;
research_platform (DNSSEC on a Grid).
